### My answer to the prompt

A reliable approach is to measure the full text without a line limit, then use the first measured line as the preview. The inline link is nested inside the main `<Text>`, so it is pressable.

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

export default function ExpandableText({
  text,
  style,
  linkStyle,
  moreLabel = 'See more',
}) {
  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);
  const [lines, setLines] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    setIsExpanded(false);
    setLines([]);
  }, [text]);

  const hasMore = lines.length > 1;

  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      {/* Measures wrapping at the same width as the visible text. */}
      <Text
        accessible={false}
        pointerEvents="none"
        onTextLayout={({ nativeEvent }) => setLines(nativeEvent.lines)}
        style={[styles.text, style, styles.measurement]}
      >
        {text}
      </Text>

      <Text style={[styles.text, style]} numberOfLines={isExpanded ? undefined : 1}>
        {isExpanded || !hasMore ? text : lines[0]?.text?.trimEnd()}
        {!isExpanded && hasMore ? (
          <Text
            accessibilityRole="button"
            onPress={() => setIsExpanded(true)}
            style={[styles.link, linkStyle]}
          >
            {' '}{moreLabel}
          </Text>
        ) : null}
      </Text>
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { position: 'relative' },
  text: { fontSize: 16, color: '#333' },
  link: { color: '#007AFF' },
  measurement: {
    position: 'absolute',
    left: 0,
    right: 0,
    opacity: 0,
  },
});
```

The link needs to fit on the first line along with the preview; for tight layouts, the preview may need additional trimming to guarantee that. The hidden measurement text must have the same width and text styles as the visible text.

### Evaluation

Assistant A is closer to the requested behavior and provides a usable component and example. However, its overflow detection is unreliable: `onTextLayout` is attached to text limited to one line, so it may not report the hidden lines. Also, appending the link after the entire original text does not ensure that “See more” appears at the end of the visible first line; truncation can hide it. Calling the answer production-ready is therefore too strong.

